{
 "turns_text": [
  "[SEG0001] person_1 (CLINICAL_STAFF): Room three's ready. Marcus, temp's 102.6, pulse 124, respirations 24, oxygen sat 97 percent, blood pressure 96 over 58. Mom's here with him.",
  "[SEG0002] person_2 (FAMILY_MEMBER): Thanks. Sorry, he's been a bear since the car, and umm the parking garage was a whole thing, we circled forever.",
  "[SEG0003] person_3 (DOCTOR): Hi Marcus, I'm Dr. Shah. Mom, what brought you guys in today?",
  "[SEG0004] person_2 (FAMILY_MEMBER): So his classmate Emma has measles, her mom texted the group, and then Marcus got a fever and now this rash, and I just didn't want to sit on it.",
  "[SEG0005] person_4 (PATIENT): I wanna go home.",
  "[SEG0006] person_3 (DOCTOR): I know, buddy, we'll keep it short. When did the fever start?",
  "[SEG0007] person_2 (FAMILY_MEMBER): Saturday. Today's Wednesday, so uh four days. He was warm Saturday night and it never really came down.",
  "[SEG0008] person_3 (DOCTOR): And the rash, when did that show up?",
  "[SEG0009] person_2 (FAMILY_MEMBER): Yesterday morning, right at the hairline on his face, and now it's down on his chest. He was already cranky and exhausted before the spots, just snappy.",
  "[SEG0010] person_4 (PATIENT): My head hurts. I'm hot. Stop talking.",
  "[SEG0011] person_3 (DOCTOR): Headache, and he feels hot. Any cough with this?",
  "[SEG0012] person_2 (FAMILY_MEMBER): Mm, I suppose. Sometimes, not really. A little throat clear, I wouldn't call it a cough.",
  "[SEG0013] person_3 (DOCTOR): Okay, I won't put cough in the note off that. Were his eyes red at all, or his nose running?",
  "[SEG0014] person_2 (FAMILY_MEMBER): Eyes look normal, he's not squinting. Nose was a little runny Monday, not today.",
  "[SEG0015] person_3 (DOCTOR): Any vomiting, diarrhea, pain when he pees?",
  "[SEG0016] person_2 (FAMILY_MEMBER): No. None of that. He's drinking water, he just won't eat, pushed the eggs away.",
  "[SEG0017] person_4 (PATIENT): Don't want eggs.",
  "[SEG0018] person_3 (DOCTOR): Fair enough. With a classmate who has measles I have to ask about the MMR. Is he up to date?",
  "[SEG0019] person_2 (FAMILY_MEMBER): Mm, I suppose. The card's in a drawer at home. School mailed something, maybe one shot was late, or maybe that was his brother, I don't actually know.",
  "[SEG0020] person_3 (DOCTOR): So we don't have a reliable vaccine history today, I'll leave it blank rather than guess. What medicines does he take every day?",
  "[SEG0021] person_4 (PATIENT): Amlodipine for the pressure. The cholesterol one at night. Sugar tablets. Only the Ayurvedic powder in the morning.",
  "[SEG0022] person_3 (DOCTOR): Okay, let me get the doses. Amlodipine how much and when, and the others?",
  "[SEG0023] person_2 (FAMILY_MEMBER): Amladipine is five milligrams, he swallows it every morning. The cholesterol one, err, I can never remember the name, it's a white oval, ten milligrams at bedtime. Sugar tablets, two every morning, I don't know the milligrams. The ayervedic powder is half a teaspoon in the morning, from the shop on Hill Street, he says it's for his tummy.",
  "[SEG0024] person_3 (DOCTOR): And those are his, not yours or a grandparent's?",
  "[SEG0025] person_2 (FAMILY_MEMBER): His, yeah. He's been on the pressure pill for a while. You didn't ask about all that old stuff so I won't start the saga.",
  "[SEG0026] person_4 (PATIENT): Powder tastes like dirt. I want my game tablet. Not the sugar one.",
  "[SEG0027] person_2 (FAMILY_MEMBER): After we leave, honey. Not in the exam room.",
  "[SEG0028] person_3 (DOCTOR): Any allergies to medicines or foods, anything that gives him a reaction?",
  "[SEG0029] person_2 (FAMILY_MEMBER): No. Nothing I know of.",
  "[SEG0030] person_3 (DOCTOR): Alright Marcus, I'm going to look in your mouth, check your eyes and your chest, and look at the rash. Big open.",
  "[SEG0031] person_3 (DOCTOR): The rash is red and blotchy over the face, the neck, and the upper chest. I do not see Koplik spots inside the cheeks. The eyes are not red. Throat's a little pink, lungs are clear, and there are a couple of small swollen nodes on both sides of the neck.",
  "[SEG0032] person_4 (PATIENT): Ow. Quit it.",
  "[SEG0033] person_3 (DOCTOR): Sorry, done with that. Let me count it back so we're on the same page. Fever started Saturday, this is day four of fever. Rash started yesterday morning, so day two of rash.",
  "[SEG0034] person_3 (DOCTOR): Measles rash often lands around day three or four of the fever, so the timing could fit. It could also be an ordinary viral rash, or roseola, except he's still hot and roseola usually drops the fever when the rash comes. I'm not going to conclude this is measles.",
  "[SEG0035] person_2 (FAMILY_MEMBER): So do you think it is, or not?",
  "[SEG0036] person_3 (DOCTOR): I think measles is possible and I can't rule it out, same for a nonspecific viral exanthem. That's a differential, not a label I'm putting on him. Let's see what the tests say.",
  "[SEG0037] person_3 (DOCTOR): I'm ordering a throat swab for measles PCR and a blood test for measles IgM. Both are going to the lab today, they're booked.",
  "[SEG0038] person_3 (DOCTOR): If the PCR is negative we might add a respiratory viral panel later, but I'm not ordering that now. Separately, measles is reportable, so I will notify the health department this afternoon about the classmate and this rash.",
  "[SEG0039] person_3 (DOCTOR): Keep him home from school and away from playdates, babies, and anyone pregnant, until I call with results. That's the isolation piece.",
  "[SEG0040] person_2 (FAMILY_MEMBER): His brother is three, they share a room, and a cup sometimes, which I hate. Also, side note, my sister's wedding RSVP is due and I haven't even picked a dress, sorry, not your problem.",
  "[SEG0041] person_2 (FAMILY_MEMBER): Oh, and last night the thermometer said 103 even. I've been giving children's Tylenol, 320 milligrams, about every six hours since Saturday when he looks awful.",
  "[SEG0042] person_3 (DOCTOR): 320 is a fine dose, stay with that, don't stack ibuprofen unless we talk. Separate the boys if you can, and no shared cups. If he's hard to wake, breathing fast, or the fever goes past 104, go to the ER, don't wait for the visit.",
  "[SEG0043] person_3 (DOCTOR): I want him back Friday, that's in two days, so I can recheck the rash. Front desk will book it, it's set.",
  "[SEG0044] person_1 (CLINICAL_STAFF): Friday at 10:15 is open. I'll put him in and print the lab slip.",
  "[SEG0045] person_2 (FAMILY_MEMBER): 10:15 works. Do I still give the morning powder with all this?",
  "[SEG0046] person_3 (DOCTOR): Yes. Keep the amlodipine, the cholesterol pill at night, the sugar tablets, and the Ayurvedic powder just as he's been taking them. Marcus, you can head out. Call us if he stops drinking."
 ],
